modify, and distribute the Software for permitted purposes.

You may use the unmodified Software for personal purposes and as an ordinary
internal tool at work, including in a business. You may use it to organize
files and create your own work products for which you are paid. Those work
products do not become subject to this license merely because Bento was used.
Noncommercial development, use, and free distribution of Modified Versions
are permitted only if you meet section 4, including for private modifications.
All rights not expressly granted are reserved.

3. Commercial exploitation requires separate permission

Without separate written permission from the relevant rights holders, you
must not:

(a) sell, rent, sublicense for a fee, or otherwise charge for copies of the
Software or a Modified Version, or condition access or downloads on payment;
(b) offer paid hosting, managed services, or other paid services that provide
users with the Software's functionality, including through a network;
(c) bundle the Software or a Modified Version as a feature of a product or
service supplied for payment, or monetize its distribution through mandatory
advertisements or equivalent consideration; or
(d) develop, commission, or supply a Modified Version as a commercial product,
for a paying client, as a revenue-generating service, or for a business's
internal operational purposes.

The ordinary internal use expressly permitted by section 2 is not prohibited
by this section. Using unmodified Bento as one of your own working tools is
different from selling access to Bento or offering its functionality as a
paid service. Voluntary donations that confer no exclusive access, features,
or other consideration are permitted.

Publishing source code does not remove these commercial restrictions.

4. Every modification requires public corresponding source

If you make any change to the Software, you must publish the complete
corresponding source of the resulting Modified Version at the time you make
that change. This obligation applies even if the change is only for your
personal use, is experimental, or is never distributed. You must publish
before running, testing, or otherwise using the Modified Version. Keeping
source changes private, including in a private repository, is not permitted.

Publish the source in a publicly accessible repository or download that
anyone can access without payment, individual approval, or an account. Keep
it available while you use, develop, offer, or distribute that Modified
Version. State the exact source revision for each binary you distribute,
and provide its public source URL with the binary and in its accompanying
notices. A link to an unmodified upstream repository alone is insufficient.

"Complete corresponding source" means the preferred editable form of all
covered code in the Modified Version, including your changes and the scripts,
configuration templates, and instructions necessary to build and install
it. Include exact dependency versions and any publicly available source
locations needed for independently licensed dependencies. Do not substitute
obfuscated code, generated output, screenshots, or a binary for source.
Independent third-party components retain their own licensing conditions.
Private signing keys, passwords, personal files, and other user data are not
required source; provide safe templates and build instructions instead.

License your covered changes under this same license, without additional
restrictions on the rights it grants. Mark your changes and preserve the
copyright, license, attribution, and third-party notices. You must not
relicense covered changes under terms that omit either section 3 or this
section. Recipients receive the rights to covered contributions directly
from their respective licensors under these terms.

5. Distribution and identity

Every distributed copy must include this license and applicable notices.
Any binary distribution must also provide the complete corresponding source
or a public, free, account-free URL to the matching source, including when
the binary is unmodified. Modified distributions must clearly identify that
they are modified and must not imply endorsement by the Bento project.

No trademark, logo, or endorsement rights are granted. This does not prevent
truthful attribution or ordinary identification of the original project.

